% YouTube live streaming

Today's quest is about creating a live stream from our church non-profit YouTube channel.



Here is the current situation:



1. Our church website has a live stream event scheduled automatically for every Sunday at 10;00.

2. This event comes from a Vimeo Live Stream.

3. The live stream itself is created live in VMIX.

4. At the end of the broadcase, the videographer has to save the file to Vimeo, otherwise it would be lost.



Here is the desired situation:



1. What do I need to do in YouTube to be able to do live streaming, through VMIX, through our website?

2. This would need to be done every Sunday, and hopefully would not required different code.

3. After the live stream is completed, the video must be saved to our channel.

4. Ideally, the video would be added to a specific playlist, if possible.



How can I do these things?

Transitioning your Sunday morning broadcasts from Vimeo to YouTube is a brilliant move for a church. It makes it completely free, increases your reach, and eliminates the biggest headache your videographer currently faces: the fear of losing the video if they forget to manually hit save.

On YouTube, saving is hands-free and completely automated. Here is the blueprint to answer all four of your goals and set up a permanent, friction-free Sunday morning pipeline.  
Elgato

1. Connecting vMix, YouTube, and Your Church Website
To make this work seamlessly, you need to establish a digital pipeline. vMix will push the video to YouTube, and your website will display that YouTube player to the congregation.

In YouTube Studio: You do not need to schedule a brand new event inside YouTube every single week. Instead, you will use what YouTube calls a Reusable Persistent Stream Key.

In vMix: Inside the vMix Streaming settings, you select YouTube Live as your destination, log into the church account once, and bind it to that persistent key. When Sunday morning arrives, the videographer literally just clicks the single "Stream" button at the bottom of the vMix screen. The video is immediately sent to YouTube.  
vMix

On Your Website: Instead of embedding a specific, single video link that changes every week, your webmaster can embed your channel’s Live Redirect URL. This is a special, permanent link layout: [youtube.com/c/](https://youtube.com/c/)[YourChannelName]/live. When someone clicks that link or loads that embedded player on your website, it will automatically detect if the church is currently live and play the stream. If you aren't live, it simply shows your channel's past videos.

2. Eliminating Weekly Code Changes (The Sunday Routine)
By utilizing that Persistent Stream Key we just mentioned, you completely eliminate the need to input new stream keys, codes, or credentials into vMix every weekend.

The setup becomes a static target:

The vMix profile stays saved with the church's permanent YouTube credentials.

Every Sunday at 9:55 AM, the videographer fires up vMix and clicks Stream.

YouTube detects the incoming data on that permanent key, automatically creates the "Sunday Service" broadcast placeholder, and begins streaming it out instantly to your website's permanent player link. No coding or settings adjustment required.

3. Automatic Archiving (No More Manual Saving)
This is the best part of the switch. Unlike Vimeo, YouTube automatically archives and saves every live stream by default.  
Elgato

The absolute moment your videographer clicks "Stop Stream" inside vMix at the end of the service, YouTube takes over on its own servers.

It immediately processes the live broadcast data into a standard, permanent video.

It automatically moves it to the public "Live" tab on your church channel page.

Zero human intervention is required. If the videographer shuts down the church computer and drives home immediately after the blessing, the video is 100% safe and will never be lost.

4. Automatically Adding the Stream to a Playlist
You can absolutely automate this so you don't have to manually sort the videos into playlists every Sunday afternoon.

1
Open Your Playlists
1 minute
Go to studio.youtube.com, click Content on the left menu, and select the Playlists tab at the top. Click on the playlist you want your Sunday services to go into (e.g., "Sunday Worship Services").

2
Access Playlist Settings
2 minutes
Click the three dots (...) menu icon beneath the playlist title and description layout, and select Playlist Settings.

3
Turn on Auto-Add Rules
2 minutes
Click on Advanced Settings and look for the tab labeled Auto-Add. Click Add Rule.

4
Define the Sunday Keyword
1 minute
Set the rule condition to: If title contains. In the text box, type a specific keyword you know will be in every single stream title (for example, Sunday Worship or St. Mary's Sermon). Click Save.


How the automation ties together: On Sunday morning, when your videographer starts the stream from vMix, they will give the stream a title like "Sunday Worship - May 31". The moment the stream ends and YouTube archives it, YouTube's system reads that title, triggers your rule because it sees the words "Sunday Worship," and automatically slips the video straight into your playlist without anyone lifting a finger.

Remember, this entire pipeline depends on your Google for Nonprofits dashboard verification being active so you have unrestricted live streaming privileges. Once that direct help ticket form we filled out earlier gets processed by Google support, you will be completely clear to build this permanent Sunday workflow!

One more question. In the present, when the videographer sets up a Sunday broadcast in VMIX, she has to click on #2 (of 3 buttons) button for Vimeo and edits the title and such. Button #1 is for YouTube. Would she have to edit the title there before starting record and stream?

The short answer is no, she will not have to edit the title every single Sunday inside vMix, provided you set up the YouTube destination using the Custom RTMP method rather than the automated login method.

Because she is already managing the Vimeo title on Button #2, adding a second title to manage on Button #1 is exactly the kind of double-work that leads to mistakes when a service is about to start.

Here is how the two different setup choices affect her Sunday routine:

Option A: The "Zero Effort" Method (Custom RTMP)
If you configure Button #1 in vMix using a Custom RTMP Server (where you input your permanent YouTube Stream URL and that Reusable Stream Key we discussed earlier):

Her Sunday Routine: She ignores Button #1 entirely. She changes the title on Button #2 for Vimeo just like she does right now, hits the master stream button, and walks away.

The Result: YouTube will accept the incoming video data perfectly. However, because vMix isn't sending a title to YouTube via this method, YouTube will automatically name the archived video whatever your default stream placeholder is called in YouTube Studio (e.g., "St. Mary's Sunday Worship"). If you want the specific date on the YouTube title (like "Sunday Worship - May 31"), someone would just need to quickly type that date into YouTube Studio at some point during the week.

Option B: The Integrated "YouTube Live" Method
If you choose to link vMix to YouTube using the built-in YouTube Live destination option (where you log into the church Google account directly inside vMix):

Her Sunday Routine: Yes, she would have to click the gear icon for Button #1, click the "YouTube Settings" button, and type the title there before pushing the stream live.

The Result: This passes the exact text title straight from vMix to YouTube's servers automatically, meaning the YouTube video will have the exact right date the second the stream finishes.

Which one should you choose?
Most church media teams prefer Option A (Custom RTMP).

It keeps the tech booth completely focused on the Vimeo/Website pipeline they already know. It ensures that even if the videographer forgets to look at the YouTube settings entirely, the stream still goes live perfectly on your website under a generic, reliable church title. You can always rename a video on YouTube after the service is over, but reducing the number of buttons a volunteer has to click right before the opening hymn is usually the biggest win!

