This changes things completely! Let’s build the entire Sunday morning broadcast pipeline from scratch.

To make this completely hands-off for your videographer, we are going to build a "Static Link Pipeline." vMix will push the stream to YouTube using a permanent "Stream Key," and your website will pull the stream using a permanent "Channel Code."

Once this is set up, no one ever has to update embed codes on WordPress, and the videographer never has to change settings inside vMix.

Here is your complete, step-by-step master plan.

Part 1: What to do in YouTube Studio (One-Time Setup)
You need to gather your unique streaming credentials and create the automated playlist rules.

Go to studio.youtube.com.

In the top right corner, click the Create button (camera icon) and select Go Live.

You will see a dark streaming dashboard. Look at the Stream Settings tab in the lower half of the screen.

Copy these two specific items and paste them into a text file (you will need them for vMix):

Stream URL

Stream Key (Click the "Eye" icon to reveal it, or just click "Copy". Keep this private!)

Ensure the toggle for "Enable Auto-start" and "Enable Auto-stop" are both turned ON. (This allows vMix to start and stop the YouTube broadcast automatically without you touching the website).

Set up the Playlist Automation: Go back to your dashboard, click Content > Playlists, select your Sunday Playlist, click the three dots (...), open Playlist Settings > Advanced, and add an Auto-Add Rule: "If title contains Sunday Worship".

Part 2: What to do in vMix (One-Time Setup)
Now we will configure Button #1 so it targets YouTube using the credentials you just copied.

Open vMix and click the Gear Icon located directly next to the word Stream at the bottom center of the screen.  
YouTube

At the very top of the Streaming Settings window, click the Square Button #1.

Change the Destination dropdown menu to read: Custom RTMP Server.

Fill out the text boxes using the items you copied from YouTube in Part 1:

In the URL box: Paste the Stream URL.

In the Stream Key box: Paste the Stream Key.

Set the Quality dropdown to 720p 3.5mbps 29.97p (or whichever 720p profile matches your camera frame rate).

Click Save and Close.

The New Sunday Routine for the Videographer:
When Sunday morning arrives, she edits the title on Button #2 for Vimeo exactly like she always does. Then, she simply hits the main Stream button. vMix will simultaneously send the stream to Vimeo (using her title) and to YouTube (using your permanent key). When the service ends, she hits Stream again to stop. YouTube will automatically stop, archive the video, and add it to your playlist. She does zero extra work.  
YouTube

Part 3: What to change on your WordPress Worship Page
Right now, your website provider is likely using an iframe embed code specifically tied to Vimeo. They need to swap it out for a Permanent YouTube Live Embed.

Tell your developer to replace the current Vimeo video player code with this exact HTML block:

HTML
<iframe 
  width="560" 
  height="315" 
  src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/live_stream?channel=YOUR_CHANNEL_ID" 
  frameborder="0" 
  allow="autoplay; encrypted-media" 
  allowfullscreen>
</iframe>
What the Developer needs to do:
They must erase the text YOUR_CHANNEL_ID inside that URL string and replace it with your church's actual YouTube Channel ID (the long code starting with UC... that we found earlier).

Save and publish the WordPress page.

Why this WordPress change is a game-changer:
This special link format tells Google to scan your channel dynamically.

When you are Live: The WordPress block automatically converts into a live broadcast player showing the service in real-time.

When you are Off-line: The player frame will automatically display a black placeholder screen or your most recent past live broadcast/upcoming schedule notice.

Your service provider only has to paste this code into WordPress one single time. They will never have to log in on a Sunday morning to update codes or change links ever again!

